{"status":"ready","content":"I need to find the determinant of this 4×4 matrix. Let me denote the matrix as A and calculate its determinant using cofactor expansion.\r\n\r\nFirst, let me write the matrix more clearly:\r\n$$A = \\begin{pmatrix}\r\n\\frac{4}{3} & 1 & -\\frac{9}{2} & -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\\r\n-\\frac{13}{3} & \\frac{25}{6} & -\\frac{19}{6} & \\frac{11}{6} \\\\\r\n-\\frac{23}{6} & \\frac{29}{6} & -\\frac{10}{3} & -\\frac{19}{6} \\\\\r\n-\\frac{17}{6} & -\\frac{7}{6} & \\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{7}{3}\r\n\\end{pmatrix}$$\r\n\r\nTo make calculations easier, I'll multiply the entire matrix by 6 to clear most fractions, then divide the final result by $6^4 = 1296$.\r\n\r\n$$6A = \\begin{pmatrix}\r\n8 & 6 & -27 & -26 \\\\\r\n-26 & 25 & -19 & 11 \\\\\r\n-23 & 29 & -20 & -19 \\\\\r\n-17 & -7 & 15 & 14\r\n\\end{pmatrix}$$\r\n\r\nNow I'll calculate $\\det(6A)$ using cofactor expansion along the first row:\r\n\r\n$\\det(6A) = 8C_{11} + 6C_{12} + (-27)C_{13} + (-26)C_{14}$\r\n\r\nWhere $C_{ij}$ are the cofactors.\r\n\r\n$C_{11} = (-1)^{1+1} \\begin{vmatrix}\r\n25 & -19 & 11 \\\\\r\n29 & -20 & -19 \\\\\r\n-7 & 15 & 14\r\n\\end{vmatrix}$\r\n\r\nLet me calculate this 3×3 determinant:\r\n$= 25(-20 \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot 15) - (-19)(29 \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot (-7)) + 11(29 \\cdot 15 - (-20) \\cdot (-7))$\r\n$= 25(-280 + 285) + 19(406 - 133) + 11(435 - 140)$\r\n$= 25(5) + 19(273) + 11(295)$\r\n$= 125 + 5187 + 3245 = 8557$\r\n\r\n$C_{12} = (-1)^{1+2} \\begin{vmatrix}\r\n-26 & -19 & 11 \\\\\r\n-23 & -20 & -19 \\\\\r\n-17 & 15 & 14\r\n\\end{vmatrix}$\r\n\r\n$= -[(-26)(-20 \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot 15) - (-19)((-23) \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot (-17)) + 11((-23) \\cdot 15 - (-20) \\cdot (-17))]$\r\n$= -[(-26)(-280 + 285) + 19(-322 - 323) + 11(-345 - 340)]$\r\n$= -[(-26)(5) + 19(-645) + 11(-685)]$\r\n$= -[-130 - 12255 - 7535] = -[-19920] = 19920$\r\n\r\n$C_{13} = (-1)^{1+3} \\begin{vmatrix}\r\n-26 & 25 & 11 \\\\\r\n-23 & 29 & -19 \\\\\r\n-17 & -7 & 14\r\n\\end{vmatrix}$\r\n\r\n$= (-26)(29 \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot (-7)) - 25((-23) \\cdot 14 - (-19) \\cdot (-17)) + 11((-23) \\cdot (-7) - 29 \\cdot (-17))$\r\n$= (-26)(406 - 133) - 25(-322 - 323) + 11(161 + 493)$\r\n$= (-26)(273) - 25(-645) + 11(654)$\r\n$= -7098 + 16125 + 7194 = 16221$\r\n\r\n$C_{14} = (-1)^{1+4} \\begin{vmatrix}\r\n-26 & 25 & -19 \\\\\r\n-23 & 29 & -20 \\\\\r\n-17 & -7 & 15\r\n\\end{vmatrix}$\r\n\r\n$= -[(-26)(29 \\cdot 15 - (-20) \\cdot (-7)) - 25((-23) \\cdot 15 - (-20) \\cdot (-17)) + (-19)((-23) \\cdot (-7) - 29 \\cdot (-17))]$\r\n$= -[(-26)(435 - 140) - 25(-345 - 340) - 19(161 + 493)]$\r\n$= -[(-26)(295) - 25(-685) - 19(654)]$\r\n$= -[-7670 + 17125 - 12426] = -[-2971] = 2971$\r\n\r\nNow: $\\det(6A) = 8(8557) + 6(19920) + (-27)(16221) + (-26)(2971)$\r\n$= 68456 + 119520 - 437967 - 77246$\r\n$= 187976 - 515213 = -327237$\r\n\r\nSince $\\det(6A) = 6^4 \\det(A)$, we have:\r\n$\\det(A) = \\frac{-327237}{1296} = -\\frac{327237}{1296}$\r\n\r\nLet me simplify this fraction by finding the GCD of 327237 and 1296.\r\n$327237 = 1296 \\cdot 252 + 645$\r\n$1296 = 645 \\cdot 2 + 6$\r\n$645 = 6 \\cdot 107 + 3$\r\n$6 = 3 \\cdot 2 + 0$\r\n\r\nSo GCD(327237, 1296) = 3.\r\n\r\n$\\det(A) = -\\frac{327237 \\div 3}{1296 \\div 3} = -\\frac{109079}{432}$\r\n\r\n$\\boxed{-\\frac{109079}{432}}$\r\n","path":"responses/claude-sonnet-4/livebench-057-math.txt","size":2981,"mtime":1779367666}